<!--
    A return type stated for a callee keeps every argument its callers pass in
    the return register. P3 heritage (`p3_dataflow/heritage.rs` guard_calls, no
    option).

    On AArch64, 32-bit ARM and PowerPC the return register is also the first
    argument register. Upstream guard_calls skips a call whose own output covers
    the heritaged range exactly, so once a qualified `map return f::%x0` locks
    the output while the inputs are still recovered, no input trial was planted
    at that register. The copy into it before the call (`mov w0,w20`) was then
    dead, and the trial added later read an older value: a saved argument
    printed as the previous call's null result and a real `0` as the caller's
    own incoming register.

    The script loads return_hint_caller_{aarch64,arm,ppc32,x86_64}.so (source
    return_hint_caller.c, recipe in the fixtures README), states the return of
    alloc_node and alloc_sub (for PowerPC32 only alloc_node, through its PIC call stub at 0x560),
    and decompiles their three callers. x86-64 returns in RAX, which is no
    argument register, and is the control.

    Assert #1: the real zero stays the first call's argument.
    Assert #2: the index kept in a callee-saved register reaches both calls.
    Assert #3: the pointer kept in a callee-saved register reaches alloc_sub.
    Assert #4: no call takes the register's earlier value.
    Assert #5 (control): with f's return stated, f in `p = g(); q = f();`
    still takes no argument, although x0 holds g's result at the call
    (return_hint_noarg_aarch64.so, source return_hint_noarg.c).
    Assert #6 (control): nor does f in `return f() + a;`, where x0 still
    holds the caller's own argument at the call.

    No upstream issue: reported against an AArch64 shared library. On the
    unpatched tree #1 to #4 fail (each AArch64, ARM and PowerPC32 call prints the
    register's earlier value) and the x86-64 control, #5 and #6 pass; with the
    fix all six pass.
-->
  <bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000" readonly="true">c0035fd6</bytechunk>
  <symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
